The Bolivian tricolor flag on the left and the steep, arid hillsides densely covered with brick and adobe buildings climbing a mountain valley are classic signs of La Paz, Bolivia. The high-altitude light, dry sparse vegetation on the slopes, and the large paved civic plaza with flagpoles fit the city's bowl-like setting. This looks like a central plaza area, possibly near Plaza Murillo or the San Francisco district.

>>>
{"country":"Bolivia","place":"La Paz","lat":-16.4955,"lng":-68.1336}

The yellow-tipped bollards, brick sidewalks, white modernist apartment blocks, and French number plates point strongly to France. In the distance the glass tower with the angular crown matches the Part-Dieu business district skyline in Lyon (Tour Oxygène/Incity cluster), seen from a residential area just to its east, so this is likely Lyon's 3rd arrondissement or nearby Villeurbanne looking west toward Part-Dieu.

>>>
{"country":"France","place":"Lyon (3rd arrondissement, near Part-Dieu)","lat":45.7567,"lng":4.8685}

Sandstone tenement blocks, left-hand driving, UK traffic lights and road studs, plus a "Funeralcare" (Co-op) shopfront all point to Scotland. The craggy hill filling the end of the street is characteristic of Arthur's Seat, which is visible straight down several north–south streets in Edinburgh's east end. The junction layout and tenement style fit the Easter Road/Abbeyhill area of Edinburgh looking toward Holyrood Park.

>>>
{"country":"United Kingdom","place":"Edinburgh, Easter Road area","lat":55.9598,"lng":-3.1726}

The bilingual sign reads "China Children's Store" (新中国儿童用品商店), which is the famous New China Children's Store on Wangfujing Street in Beijing. The red Lunar New Year couplets reference the Year of the Snake, and the surrounding Longines boutique and 24h self-service bank match the Wangfujing pedestrian shopping district. This is a well-known landmark at 168 Wangfujing Dajie.

>>>
{"country":"China","place":"Wangfujing Street, Beijing","lat":39.9130,"lng":116.4108}

PART 1: The platform display reads "ANCONA" with train "RV4156 15:55" in Trenitalia's regional format, placing this firmly at an Italian station on the Adriatic corridor south of Ancona. The Trenitalia regional train, platform furniture, clock style, catenary, and signage fonts are all classic Italian FS design, and the parked scooters and paving suit a mid-size Adriatic coastal city. The largest station fitting this layout on the line toward Ancona is Pescara Centrale.

PART 2:
>>> {"country":"Italy","place":"Pescara Centrale, Pescara","lat":42.4632,"lng":14.2135}

PART 1: The strongest clues are the sari-sari style roadside store plastered with product ads, the motorcycle/tricycle, the red octagonal stop sign, and the concrete barangay road with a center seam, all framed by dense tropical vegetation (banana, palms) and Filipino-style utility poles. These point firmly to the Philippines, most likely a provincial road on Luzon rather than a city. The bright overhead sun and lush greenery fit a humid lowland barangay in the CALABARZON/Quezon side of southern Luzon.

>>>
{"country":"Philippines","place":"provincial Luzon, near Lucena–Sariaya, Quezon","lat":13.9620,"lng":121.5260}

The hand-set cobblestones (paralelepípedo), tall stone retaining walls draped in Atlantic-forest vegetation, and steep uphill lane are signature features of Rio de Janeiro's hilltop Santa Teresa district. The white Mercedes Sprinter with a blurred Brazilian plate and the colonial-style gates and balustrades reinforce a Brazilian, specifically Rio, setting.

>>>
{"country":"Brazil","place":"Santa Teresa, Rio de Janeiro","lat":-22.9178,"lng":-43.1863}

PART 1: The sign "FOTOGRAFI" is Scandinavian/Icelandic, and the red corrugated-iron building is classic Reykjavík architecture. The black-and-white photos of sheep in a mossy landscape strongly suggest Iceland. This looks like the small photo gallery/shop in downtown Reykjavík near Laugavegur.

>>>
{"country":"Iceland","place":"Reykjavik","lat":64.1455,"lng":-21.9300}

PART 1: The red Bajaj auto-rickshaw, left-hand traffic, and tropical urban streetscape strongly suggest Sri Lanka. English billboard text ("TEXSTRETCH") fits Sri Lanka's widespread English signage, and the distinctive mushroom-shaped water tower plus dense commercial buildings along a divided arterial road point to greater Colombo.

PART 2:
>>> {"country":"Sri Lanka","place":"Colombo","lat":6.8937,"lng":79.8612}

This is a nightlife strip with English-language tattoo parlour signs ("BIGGA INK TATTOO"), open-air bars, red crowd barriers and shophouse architecture typical of Bangkok's backpacker district. The distinctive lattice-roofed venue is a well-known club entrance on Khao San Road, and the tropical vegetation, building style and street furniture all fit central Bangkok, Thailand.

>>>
{"country":"Thailand","place":"Khao San Road, Bangkok","lat":13.7587,"lng":100.4974}

PART 1: Right-hand driving on a multi-lane European motorway with overhead gantries displaying 70 km/h speed limits points strongly to Belgium, where such 70 zones are typical on urban ring motorways. The yellow "LUX" curtainsider is consistent with a Benelux haulier, and the flat green landscape plus gantry style match the Antwerp/Brussels ring corridors. I'll place it on the Antwerp Ring (R1) area.

>>>
{"country":"Belgium","place":"Antwerp (R1 ring motorway)","lat":51.2194,"lng":4.4025}

The scene shows a surface parking lot beneath a metal canopy, ringed by mid- and high-rise Mediterranean/Middle-Eastern towers, including a distinctive white glass residential high-rise with side balconies and a pink rooftop sign on a nearby hotel-style block. The hazy warm light, pale plates, red fire hydrant, and dense mixed-era towers fit central Tel Aviv rather than the Gulf or Latin America. I place it in Tel Aviv near the northern central business/Arlozorov edge.

>>>
{"country":"Israel","place":"Tel Aviv-Yafo","lat":32.0870,"lng":34.7890}

The mix of weathered Soviet-era stone apartment blocks with ornate balconies alongside a modern curved glass building, the broad tree-shaded pedestrian median, and the lush walnut-like canopy under a dry, intense sun strongly suggest a Caucasus capital — most likely Yerevan, Armenia, which is famous for such leafy avenues and this architectural blend.

>>>
{"country":"Armenia","place":"Yerevan","lat":40.1811,"lng":44.5136}

The building on the right is clearly labeled "WIEN MITTE," and the glass complex on the left advertises "CAT City Airport Train" with "16 min airport City Check-in" — this is the Wien Mitte/Landstraße station area in Vienna, Austria, where the City Airport Train departs. The distinctive angular gold-glass facade of the Wien Mitte mall and the street layout at the Landstraßer Hauptstraße/Invalidenstraße junction confirm the spot.

>>>
{"country":"Austria","place":"Vienna, Wien Mitte (Landstraße)","lat":48.2062,"lng":16.3838}

PART 1: The red billboard on the right is written in Croatian (Latin script, with a date like "29. travnja"), and the blue directional sign plus right-hand driving fit Croatia. The Austro-Hungarian-style plastered buildings with chimneys and the forested hill on the horizon are typical of Zagreb, where broad avenues like this run toward the Medvednica foothills.

PART 2:
>>> {"country":"Croatia","place":"Zagreb","lat":45.8131,"lng":15.9775}

Traditional Chinese signage, the ubiquitous Taiwanese "美而美" breakfast chain, scooter-lined streets and Taiwan-style green road signs and horizontal traffic lights all point firmly to Taiwan. The shop sign on the left shows a phone number beginning with 06, the Tainan/Chiayi area code, and mentions "東山" (Dongshan), suggesting Dongshan District in Tainan.

>>>
{"country":"Taiwan","place":"Dongshan District, Tainan","lat":23.3242,"lng":120.4038}

PART 1: The left-hand traffic, white Toyota minivan, transparent noise barriers on an elevated expressway, and Japanese-style road signage (yellow warning sign visible in the distance) all point to Japan. The dense urban elevated highway with overhead high-voltage lines and mid-rise city buildings is characteristic of the Hanshin Expressway network around Osaka.

PART 2:
>>> {"country":"Japan","place":"Osaka","lat":34.6937,"lng":135.5023}

The pink VOI e-scooter, cobblestone quayside, and the harbour setting point to Sweden, and the red-pillared modern building on the left is the Gothenburg Opera House on Packhusplatsen. The corten-steel arch sculpture and view across the Göta älv toward Hisingen confirm this is Gothenburg's waterfront.

>>>
{"country":"Sweden","place":"Gothenburg, Packhuskajen near the Opera House","lat":57.7089,"lng":11.9643}

The billboard text is mirrored Malagasy reading "IANAO NO BOSS" and "1 TAONA" (1 year) — "Ianao" and "taona" are distinctly Malagasy words, pointing to Madagascar. Right-hand driving, dense motorcycle traffic, tropical vegetation like bougainvillea, the concrete utility pole style, and mobile-money/data ads ("1Go") all fit the capital's urban arterials. This looks like a main road in Antananarivo.

>>>
{"country":"Madagascar","place":"Antananarivo","lat":-18.8792,"lng":47.5079}

The scene shows socialist-era prefab apartment blocks with external AC units, typical of former Yugoslavia. The European no-stopping sign with a red-text supplementary plate below matches Serbian road signage ("zabrana stajanja i parkiranja"), and the bare deciduous trees plus mild winter light fit Belgrade. The layout with the central drainage channel in the asphalt is common in the Novi Beograd housing blocks.

>>>
{"country":"Serbia","place":"Novi Beograd, Belgrade","lat":44.8066,"lng":20.4063}

PART 1: The sign reads "Boulevard Abdou..." in French, the billboard advertises "MEGA TERAL" (a Senegalese real estate developer), and plates are Senegalese format with right-hand driving. The arid sandy roadside, sahelian vegetation, and construction cranes point to Dakar's fast-growing outskirts, likely toward Diamniadio.

PART 2:
>>> {"country":"Senegal","place":"Dakar (Diamniadio corridor)","lat":14.7260,"lng":-17.3760}

PART 1: The strongest clues are the yellow taxi, the red flag with what appears to be a white crescent on the modern glass building (Turkish flag), and the European KNAUS-badged Fiat Ducato campervan, all pointing to Turkey. The flat, dusty steppe landscape, intense sun, wide concrete boulevards, and a skyline crowded with tower cranes fit a booming Central Anatolian city rather than coastal Turkey. This combination of new development and arid openness suggests the outskirts of Ankara.

PART 2:
>>> {"country":"Turkey","place":"Ankara","lat":39.9208,"lng":32.8410}

PART 1: The red-brick cycle path, triangular give-way sign with an end-of-zone plate above it, Dutch-style bollards, a cyclist, and a yellow Dutch licence plate all point firmly to the Netherlands. The large villa with ornate gables set in mature parkland suggests an affluent town in the Gooi region such as Hilversum or Baarn, known for such 19th-century country houses.

PART 2:
>>> {"country":"Netherlands","place":"Hilversum, North Holland","lat":52.2292,"lng":5.1669}

PART 1: The curved grey giwa roof tiles, dark brick walls, and red wooden beams with white circular end caps are classic Joseon-era Korean architecture. The green sign bears the Chinese characters "景福" (Gyeongbok), the first two characters of 景福宮 — Gyeongbokgung Palace. This points to the palace complex or its adjacent hanok streets in central Seoul, South Korea.

PART 2:
>>> {"country":"South Korea","place":"Gyeongbokgung Palace, Seoul","lat":37.5796,"lng":126.9770}

