>>> {"country":"Germany","place":"Berlin (Charlottenburg, near Kurfürstendamm)","lat":52.5025,"lng":13.3265}

The black taxis with yellow roofs are classic Santiago de Chile, and the blue van resembles a Chilean colectivo. The cream twin-towered church, wide paved plaza, and tree-lined divided avenue fit the Providencia/Baquedano area near Plaza Italia, with a Chilean flag visible on the right.

>>>
{"country":"Chile","place":"Santiago, Plaza Baquedano (Plaza Italia) area","lat":-33.4372,"lng":-70.6347}

PART 1: The green "WOLNE" sign is Polish for "available/free" parking spaces, and the parking signage style with the blue "P" boards is characteristic of Polish cities. The bare deciduous trees, brick tenement architecture, grey construction hoarding with graffiti, and overcast winter look fit a large central Polish city. Given the mix of old brick tenements and active infill construction, this looks like central Kraków.

PART 2:
>>> {"country":"Poland","place":"Kraków","lat":50.0647,"lng":19.9450}

The date-palm-lined roundabout, manicured hedges, hazy desert sky and black-and-yellow painted curbs are classic Arabian Gulf streetscaping. The blue "keep right" mandatory sign, double-arm lamp posts and right-hand traffic fit the UAE specifically, and the distant canopy and utility kiosk suggest an airport or institutional approach road. The flat terrain and dense irrigated planting point away from mountainous Oman and toward the Abu Dhabi emirate's inland garden city of Al Ain.

>>>
{"country":"United Arab Emirates","place":"Al Ain, Abu Dhabi Emirate","lat":24.2075,"lng":55.7447}

PART 1: The two European motorhomes (one clearly a German "Sunlight" brand), the European-style "no entry" sign, and the vast flat parking area beside a shallow lagoon all point to a camper rest area on Italy's northern Adriatic coast. The pancake-flat terrain, hazy lagoon water, and low distant shoreline are classic Venetian Lagoon scenery, where large RV car parks serve ferry terminals to Venice. This strongly resembles the big parking lots at Punta Sabbioni in Cavallino-Treporti, a major motorhome and ferry hub.

PART 2:
>>> {"country":"Italy","place":"Punta Sabbioni, Cavallino-Treporti, Veneto","lat":45.4410,"lng":12.4250}

The signs are in Spanish with prices like "4.995" using the thousands-dot format typical of Costa Rican colones, and "Avenida Cero" is a well-known shop on San José's pedestrian Avenida Central. The pigeon-filled promenade, ornate iron kiosk and lamp posts match the walkway by Parque Central in downtown San José.

>>>
{"country":"Costa Rica","place":"San José, Avenida Central","lat":9.9335,"lng":-84.0795}

The wide seaside promenade lined with balconied mid-rise apartment blocks, the ornate green railings, bollards, and the Lime e-scooter strongly suggest a Greek city waterfront. The layout—road beside a broad pedestrian walkway curving along a calm gulf with the sun setting over the water—matches Thessaloniki's Nea Paralia along Leoforos Nikis, facing the Thermaic Gulf. The shop signage and architectural style reinforce a northern Greek urban setting.

>>>
{"country":"Greece","place":"Thessaloniki, Nea Paralia waterfront","lat":40.6291,"lng":22.9505}

The green storefront sign is written in Urdu, which points strongly to Pakistan. The streetscape—tangled overhead power lines on concrete poles, weathered mid-rise concrete buildings, the woman in a colorful dupatta, and the small white Japanese-style truck—fits an older residential-commercial neighborhood of a major Pakistani city. Traffic appears to keep left, consistent with Pakistan. The dusty, sun-baked feel and mature street trees suggest somewhere like inner Lahore rather than the denser coastal fabric of Karachi.

>>>
{"country":"Pakistan","place":"Lahore","lat":31.5497,"lng":74.3436}

Traditional Chinese signage advertising jade, ceramics and art goods (玉石陶瓷藝品, 愛玉園) points strongly to Taiwan, and jade shops like this are a hallmark of Hualien, Taiwan's jade capital. The palm-lined divided boulevard, right-hand traffic, scooters, and the high mountain wall of the Central Range looming at the end of the street all fit Hualien City's grid of wide avenues. Concrete mid-rise buildings with metal window grilles and roller shutters are typical Taiwanese urban streetscape.

>>>
{"country":"Taiwan","place":"Hualien City","lat":23.9871,"lng":121.6012}

Whitewashed tree trunks, red soil, red-painted curbs, and rows of palms point strongly to Morocco, where painting tree bases white is a widespread practice. The wide divided roadway with ornate lamp posts and the ochre-tinted median kerb is very characteristic of Marrakech's outskirts, where roads are lined with whitewashed trees and the soil is distinctly reddish.

>>>
{"country":"Morocco","place":"Marrakech","lat":31.6175,"lng":-8.0210}

The white Toyota taxi with a yellow roof light and colorful wave livery is classic Abu Dhabi (UAE) fleet styling, and the right-hand traffic, date-palm median, manicured white-flower beds, and grand multi-lane boulevard all fit the capital. The mature tree belt on the right resembles the Corniche/Umm Al Emarat park edge, so I’m placing it on a main Abu Dhabi avenue near the Corniche.
>>>
{"country":"United Arab Emirates","place":"Abu Dhabi, Corniche area","lat":24.4667,"lng":54.3667}

PART 1: The sign "PLATS NR 31" is Swedish for "Place No. 31", pointing squarely to Sweden. The permanent metal market stalls selling cut flowers on fan-patterned granite setts match the famous daily flower market at Hötorget square in central Stockholm, and the surrounding commercial buildings fit that downtown setting.

>>>
{"country":"Sweden","place":"Hötorget, Stockholm","lat":59.3349,"lng":18.0629}

The lead-grey Ottoman dome with a golden alem, the dense hillside urban fabric, and the yellow city bus on an elevated stone-walled road all point to Istanbul. The foreground Fiat Egea, Renault/Ford/Mercedes mix and Turkish-format plates confirm Turkey, and the Bosphorus-side setting fits the European shore around Beşiktaş–Kabataş.

>>>
{"country":"Turkey","place":"Istanbul, Beşiktaş (European Bosphorus shore)","lat":41.0410,"lng":29.0040}

The sign "…UGSVEIEN 31" reads Hegdehaugsveien 31, and the tenant board lists Norwegian firms like Kredinor and CEMAsys, pinpointing Oslo, Norway. Tram tracks, overhead catenary, right-hand traffic, and the wet Nordic streetscape confirm this is the Bogstadveien/Majorstuen area looking along the tram line.

>>>
{"country":"Norway","place":"Majorstuen, Oslo","lat":59.9293,"lng":10.7155}

The dense trolleybus catenary with wire frogs, weathered concrete utility poles carrying many lines, and the round red-bordered speed-limit sign with a “200 m” distance plate all point to a post-Soviet, right-hand-traffic country, most likely Russia. The wide divided arterial, birch greenery, worn asphalt and billboard style fit a large regional Russian city rather than Moscow. I’ll place it in Yekaterinburg, a major trolleybus city with avenues of exactly this character.
>>>
{"country":"Russia","place":"Yekaterinburg","lat":56.8389,"lng":60.6057}

The rooftop solar water heaters, whitewashed mid-rise apartment blocks with balconies and satellite dishes, plus palm trees alongside a tall cypress, strongly suggest a Mediterranean Turkish city. The van's red logo and European-style plates fit Turkey, and the vegetation rules out inland regions, pointing to the Antalya–Mersin coast.

>>>
{"country":"Turkey","place":"Antalya","lat":36.8841,"lng":30.7056}

The scene shows a Baroque castle courtyard with distinctive deep-red plastered walls, mansard roofs, and Central European architecture, with summer tourists strolling a gravel forecourt. This strongly matches Mikulov Castle (Zámek Mikulov) in South Moravia, Czech Republic, famous for its red-painted facade and courtyard near the town's wine district. The building styles and roof forms fit Moravian Baroque rather than Austrian or German equivalents.

>>>
{"country":"Czech Republic","place":"Mikulov","lat":48.8064,"lng":16.6377}

Red silhouette sculptures line a riverside promenade with a public electric BBQ in the foreground, a hallmark of Australian parks, and a subtropical city skyline rises across the wide river. This is the Brisbane River with the Brisbane CBD viewed from the Kangaroo Point side, matching the tower cluster and the riverside boardwalk with its public art installation.

>>>
{"country":"Australia","place":"Kangaroo Point, Brisbane, Queensland","lat":-27.4778,"lng":153.0355}

The whimsical mural of a girl on a bicycle is characteristic of Julieta XLF, a street artist whose work is concentrated in Valencia's El Carmen district. The ochre walls, wrought-iron balconies, barred windows, and brick-paved alley all match the historic Ciutat Vella streetscape of Valencia, Spain.

>>>
{"country":"Spain","place":"El Carmen, Valencia","lat":39.4795,"lng":-0.3790}

The stone-slab paving, ochre façades with rusticated bases, arched dark-green *portone* numbered 36, brass entry plate and overhead tram/trolley wires are all strongly characteristic of a historic northern-Italian city, most likely Milan’s Brera/historic-centre lanes. The Mediterranean vegetation and Italian “passo carrabile” style door sticker reinforce Italy rather than elsewhere in Europe.

>>>
{"country":"Italy","place":"Milan, Brera district","lat":45.4715,"lng":9.1880}

PART 1: The red box is a classic "Little Free Library," which is most common in North America, and the wide paved path with rustic wood-slat fencing resembles a US urban greenway or rail-trail. Bare deciduous trees with mild-looking shrubbery suggest a temperate southern or mid-latitude US city in winter, and the modern lit buildings behind hint at a redeveloped district like Atlanta's BeltLine. With no readable signs or plates, I'll commit to that corridor.

PART 2:
>>> {"country":"United States","place":"Atlanta, Georgia (BeltLine Eastside Trail)","lat":33.7720,"lng":-84.3630}

The scene shows a wide multi-lane avenue with overhead trolleybus/tram wires, a grassy tree-lined median, a classic 5-story khrushchevka apartment block with curved corner balconies on the right, and tall brick residential towers in the distance — all hallmarks of a large Russian city. The birch/poplar vegetation, overcast light, and road furniture suggest the Urals or Siberia rather than Moscow or the south. Best fit is a major Ural city such as Yekaterinburg.

>>>
{"country":"Russia","place":"Yekaterinburg","lat":56.8389,"lng":60.6057}

The brick houses with distinctive stepped (crow-step) gables are classic Flemish architecture, and the cobblestone street, white EU plates, and right-hand parking all point to Belgium. The park-lined street with old almshouse-style row houses is very characteristic of Bruges, near the Koningin Astridpark / Beguinage area.

>>>
{"country":"Belgium","place":"Bruges","lat":51.2040,"lng":3.2220}

The wide multi-lane motorway with long white dashed markings, dark panel noise barrier, right-hand traffic, and flat green landscape with mixed deciduous trees strongly suggests a German Autobahn. The noise barrier style and road design are typical of northern/central Germany, with flat terrain pointing toward Lower Saxony or the North German Plain.

>>>
{"country":"Germany","place":"Hanover, Lower Saxony","lat":52.3759,"lng":9.7320}

PART 1: This is clearly a US suburban office park: right-hand traffic, red fire-lane curbs, and a parked Lime e-scooter. The monument sign reads "Gateway Plaza," and the modern white office block with a blue corporate sign plus lush lawns and mature deciduous trees points to an established business park in the Intermountain West, with Boise, Idaho's ParkCenter office corridor (home to healthcare/corporate campuses) being a strong match.

PART 2:
>>> {"country":"United States","place":"Boise, Idaho (ParkCenter office area)","lat":43.5999,"lng":-116.2031}

The tall Norfolk Island pines, right-hand-drive utes, and coastal car park point strongly to New Zealand. The white building with radio antenna masts matches the historic Musick Memorial Radio Station at Musick Point, a well-known coastal reserve near Bucklands Beach in east Auckland.

>>>
{"country":"New Zealand","place":"Musick Point, Bucklands Beach, Auckland","lat":-36.8616,"lng":174.8994}

The red-brick and dark-timber house on the left is a classic Northern German farmhouse style, typical of Schleswig-Holstein or Lower Saxony. The right-hand traffic, concrete slab sidewalks, wide asphalt road, and lush deciduous trees all fit a suburban/rural fringe of northern Germany. The mix of traditional and modern buildings suggests a village near a larger city like Hamburg.

>>>
{"country":"Germany","place":"Schleswig-Holstein, north of Hamburg","lat":53.8500,"lng":10.0500}

The posters are in Sinhala script with typical Sri Lankan tuition-class advertisement style, pinning this to Sri Lanka. The tropical vegetation, coconut palms, brick-paved residential lane, tiled roofs, and dense tangle of utility wires all fit a suburban area in Sri Lanka's wet zone. The overall character matches a middle-class suburb of Colombo rather than the drier north or the hill country.

>>>
{"country":"Sri Lanka","place":"Colombo suburbs (Moratuwa area)","lat":6.7735,"lng":79.8822}

The architecture—detached gabled houses with grey-rendered bases, the brick boundary wall with small cellar windows, and wooden utility poles—fits a typical Central European village, most likely Czechia or Slovakia. The straight regional road with white edge lines and grassy verges, plus the flat agricultural landscape, points to the Czech countryside rather than mountainous areas. Without readable plates or signs, a village setting in the Central Bohemian lowlands is the best fit.

>>>
{"country":"Czechia","place":"Central Bohemia, near Prague","lat":50.0755,"lng":14.4378}

PART 1: Right-hand traffic, European-style guardrails, road markings, and lattice high-voltage pylons point to Central Europe. The broad green valley flanked by forested Alpine slopes strongly suggests a Swiss or Austrian motorway corridor, most likely the Swiss Rhine valley (A13) near Sargans/Bad Ragaz heading toward Chur.

PART 2:
>>> {"country":"Switzerland","place":"Rhine Valley near Bad Ragaz, canton St. Gallen","lat":47.0350,"lng":9.4700}

The stone-and-timber alpine architecture, the reversed rooftop "HOTEL" sign, and the facade branding resembling "Abba Xalet" point to the Abba Xalet Suites Hotel in Sispony, Andorra, a well-known mountain hotel in the Pyrenees. Tall conifers, the steep dark mountainside, and the Pyrenean resort style all fit Andorra.

>>>
{"country":"Andorra","place":"Sispony, La Massana","lat":42.5338,"lng":1.5132}

The arid scrubland with tall columnar cacti, divi-divi-like trees, and intense tropical sun point strongly to the ABC islands in the southern Caribbean. The modest concrete houses with bright red metal roofs and yellow trim, along with the simple paved rural road and utility pole style, are very characteristic of Curaçao's countryside. The flat, dry terrain with low hills fits the western part of the island.
>>>
{"country":"Curaçao","place":"Western Curaçao, near Sint Willibrordus","lat":12.2167,"lng":-69.0667}

The steep forested Pyrenean slopes rising directly behind dense mid-rise apartment blocks are classic for Andorra's narrow valleys. The stone-clad and brightly painted residential towers, right-hand traffic, Catalan-style street furniture, and the busy main road fit the Andorra la Vella–Escaldes-Engordany urban corridor, with the side valley dropping away to the right matching the Valira valley geography.

>>>
{"country":"Andorra","place":"Escaldes-Engordany","lat":42.5087,"lng":1.5386}

The double solid white center line, white edge dashes, and especially the yellow roadside delineator posts are classic Icelandic highway features. The treeless, moss-green rolling hills, scattered farm buildings, and overcast subarctic light fit rural Iceland rather than Scotland or Scandinavia. This looks like a stretch of Route 1 in northern Iceland's farmland valleys, around the Varmahlíð/Blönduós area.

>>>
{"country":"Iceland","place":"Route 1 near Varmahlíð, Northwest Iceland","lat":65.61,"lng":-19.44}

PART 1: Right-hand traffic with European-style triangular yield/warning signs points to continental Europe. The single-storey houses with red tile roofs, the ochre Catholic church tower at the end of the street, concrete utility poles, and flat Pannonian terrain are classic for a Hungarian or Vojvodina-style village. Solar-panel street lighting and renovated concrete fence pillars suggest recent EU-funded village infrastructure, common in Hungary's Great Plain. I'll commit to a village in central-eastern Hungary.

